Statistical Analysis
To examine how aspiration duration, onset consonant identity, and vowel context influenced listeners' perception of word-final consonant voicing, we fit a Bayesian mixed-effects logistic regression model using the brms package (version X.X.X; Bürkner, 2017) in R (version X.X.X; R Core Team, 2023). The binary outcome variable was participants' forced-choice voicing response (coded as 1 for voiced, 0 for voiceless).
Fixed effects. The model included main effects of stimulus length (short vs. long aspiration, with long as the reference level), onset consonant (p, t, k, with k as the reference level), and vowel (a, e, i, with a as the reference level), along with all possible two-way and three-way interactions among these factors. This full factorial design allowed us to assess whether aspiration duration effects varied across different phonetic contexts.
Random effects. Because all participants responded to all combinations of experimental conditions (fully within-subjects design), we included a maximal random effects structure (Barr et al., 2013) with by-subject random intercepts and random slopes for all main effects and interactions: (1 + stim_length * ons_con * vowel | sub). This structure accounts for individual differences not only in baseline voicing bias but also in how each participant weighted aspiration duration and phonetic context in their perceptual judgments. Correlations among random effects were estimated, allowing the model to capture, for example, whether participants with a strong overall voicing bias also showed stronger aspiration length effects.
Estimation and convergence. We used weakly informative default priors provided by brms. Posterior distributions were estimated via Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) sampling using the No-U-Turn Sampler (NUTS; Hoffman & Gelman, 2014), with 4 chains of 2,000 iterations each (1,000 warmup per chain), yielding 4,000 post-warmup samples. Convergence diagnostics indicated successful estimation: all R̂ values equaled 1.00 (values < 1.01 indicate convergence; Vehtari et al., 2021), and effective sample sizes were adequate for all parameters (all bulk ESS > 900, all tail ESS > 800).
Inference. We report posterior mean estimates and 95% credible intervals (CIs) for all fixed effects on the log-odds scale. A 95% credible interval indicates the range within which the true parameter value lies with 95% probability given the data and model. We consider effects credible when their 95% CIs exclude zero, though we emphasize interpreting effect sizes and uncertainty rather than relying solely on whether intervals exclude zero (Gelman et al., 2020). For ease of interpretation, we also present selected predictions transformed to the probability scale.


