## Alternative: More Concise Version

## Results

Listeners showed a strong compensatory perceptual pattern: short aspiration substantially reduced voiced coda responses compared to long aspiration (β = -1.41, 95% CI [-2.67, -0.22]). This indicates that longer aspiration in the onset—characteristic of voiceless stops—led listeners to infer a voiced final consonant, consistent with coarticulatory compensation strategies.

Onset consonant identity modulated these perceptual patterns, particularly in interaction with vowel context. The /p/ + /e/ combination substantially increased voiced responses relative to /k/ + /a/ (β = 1.96, 95% CI [0.61, 3.38]), with a similar though marginal pattern for /p/ + /i/ (β = 1.24, 95% CI [-0.19, 2.73]). The aspiration length effect showed a marginal interaction with onset consonant (β = 1.13, 95% CI [-0.28, 2.59]), suggesting slightly reduced sensitivity to aspiration cues for /p/ versus /k/. Neither three-way interaction was credible.

Individual differences were substantial and theoretically informative. Participants varied most in their sensitivity to aspiration length (SD = 1.60, 95% CI [0.76, 2.63]), indicating considerable heterogeneity in compensatory perceptual strategies despite the clear population-level effect. Baseline voicing bias also showed marked individual variation (SD = 1.32, 95% CI [0.76, 2.05]), while individual differences in other effects were more modest.

